---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security policy and vulnerability reporting"
  description="The mod_pagespeed 2.1 security policy: how to report a vulnerability, the threat model, supply chain security and safe harbor for researchers."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h1 class="h1-hero text-text-body">Security policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-text-muted">
      mod_pagespeed 2.1 processes untrusted content &mdash; HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images from
      origin servers. We take this seriously.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10">
      <!-- Reporting vulnerabilities -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Reporting vulnerabilities</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          If you've found a security vulnerability, please email
          <a
            href="mailto:security@we-amp.com"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
            >security@we-amp.com</a
          >
          with details. Do not open a public GitHub issue for security vulnerabilities.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- What to include -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">What to include</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-text-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>A description of the vulnerability and its potential impact.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Steps to reproduce the issue.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Affected versions, if known.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Your assessment of severity and impact.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Our commitment -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Our commitment</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We acknowledge reports within 72 hours and follow a 90-day coordinated disclosure window:
          we aim to release a fix within 90 days of the initial report, after which the reporter may
          publish details. Critical vulnerabilities affecting production deployments jump the queue.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Safe harbor -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Safe harbor</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We will not pursue legal action against security researchers who act in good faith. Good
          faith means: you report the vulnerability to us before disclosing publicly, you don't
          access or modify other users' data, and you don't degrade the service for others.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Scope -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Scope</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          This security policy covers the mod_pagespeed 2.1 software (the module, the optimizer
          worker, and the cache layer) on Apache, nginx and IIS, and the
          <a
            href="https://modpagespeed.com"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">modpagespeed.com</a
          > website.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Security updates -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Security updates</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Every security fix is listed on the
          <a
            href="/docs/release-notes/#security-updates"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">release notes</a
          >, with the release that carried it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Evidence -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Evidence</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Security disclosure</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              Report vulnerabilities to
              <a
                href="mailto:security@we-amp.com"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >security@we-amp.com</a
              >. Reports can go by email; the SECURITY.md in our repositories is the canonical policy. See
              <a
                href="/.well-known/security.txt"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">security.txt</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">CVE history</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              Tracked in the
              <a
                href="/docs/release-notes/#security-updates"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">release notes</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Dependency audits</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              The dependency gate blocks a change on any fixable vulnerability of medium severity or
              higher, checked against a pinned vulnerability database, and a daily scan against the
              live feed catches newly published ones.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">SBOM</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              An SPDX 2.3 SBOM for the 2.1 line, with a VEX document, is published in the <a
                href="https://github.com/We-Amp/pagespeed-optimizer/tree/main/sbom"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                ><code>sbom/</code> directory</a
              > of the source tree. The apt and yum repositories are GPG-signed and every release asset
              is listed in <code>SHA256SUMS</code>. What hardened builds add is on the <a
                href="/support/#hardened-builds"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">support page</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Architecture note -->
      <div class="panel">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-body">Architecture note</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Both parts process untrusted content by design &mdash; they rewrite HTML, minify CSS and
          JavaScript, and transcode images from origins you configure. We take this attack surface
          seriously. Both parts of mod_pagespeed 2.1 build on a current toolchain and run with
          AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer in CI, catching memory safety issues and
          undefined behavior before they reach production. The optimizer worker is written in C++23
          with smart pointers and RAII throughout.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Threat model -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Threat model</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          mod_pagespeed processes untrusted images, CSS, JS, and HTML from origin servers. The
          worker process communicates only via a local Unix socket &mdash; it has no network access
          beyond the cache file and socket. All content parsing uses memory-safe patterns validated
          by continuous sanitizer testing (ASan, UBSan, TSan). No user data is transmitted
          externally.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Supply chain -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Supply chain</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          All dependencies are pinned with SHA-256 checksums in the build configuration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
